# Security Rules

## Responsibilities

- Protect client-attorney privileged data and PII
- Enforce authentication, authorization, and audit requirements
- Prevent OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities across all layers

## Best Practices

- **Auth**: NextAuth.js v5 (Auth.js) with secure session cookies
- **RBAC**: Role → Permission matrix; check permissions, not roles, in code
- **Multi-tenant isolation**: organizationId filter on every data query
- **Password**: bcrypt/argon2, min 12 chars, breach check optional
- **Session**: HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax cookies; rotation on privilege change
- **2FA**: TOTP support (Phase 2 milestone)
- **Rate limiting**: Login, password reset, API webhooks
- **CSP headers** via Next.js config
- **Dependency scanning** in CI (npm audit, Snyk)

## Coding Standards

```typescript
// Permission check pattern
await requirePermission(session, 'cases:update');

// Organization scope check
const caseRecord = await caseRepository.findById(id, session.organizationId);
if (!caseRecord) throw new NotFoundError(); // don't reveal existence across tenants
```

- Hash API keys; store only prefix for display
- Encrypt sensitive settings at rest (AES-256-GCM)
- Log security events: failed login, permission denied, data export
- Sanitize HTML with DOMPurify before render

## Folder Structure

```
src/server/auth/          # Auth config, session helpers
src/server/permissions/   # Permission definitions and checks
src/middleware.ts         # Route protection
```

## Naming Convention

- Permissions: `resource:action` → `cases:read`, `invoices:void`
- Roles: PascalCase | `ADMIN`, `ATTORNEY`, `PARALEGAL`, `BILLING`, `CLIENT_PORTAL`

## Performance Rules

- Cache permission sets per session (invalidate on role change)
- Avoid N+1 permission checks — load once per request

## Security Rules (Detailed)

| Threat | Mitigation |
|--------|------------|
| SQL Injection | Prisma parameterized queries only |
| XSS | React escaping + DOMPurify for rich text |
| CSRF | Server Actions + SameSite cookies |
| IDOR | organizationId + ownership checks |
| Broken Auth | Secure sessions, lockout after failed attempts |
| Mass Assignment | Zod schemas whitelist fields |
| File Upload | MIME validation, size limits, virus scan (Phase 2) |
| Secrets | Environment variables, never in client bundle |

## Examples

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — Fail closed
export async function requireAuth() {
  const session = await auth();
  if (!session?.user?.id) redirect('/login');
  return session;
}

// ❌ BAD — Trust client-provided organizationId
async function getCases(organizationId: string) {
  return prisma.case.findMany({ where: { organizationId } }); // from client input!
}
```

## Things to Avoid

- Storing passwords in plain text or reversible encryption
- Client-side authorization checks without server validation
- Exposing internal error details to users
- `eval`, dynamic code execution
- Logging PII or credentials

## Checklist Before Finishing Any Task

- [ ] Server-side auth check on all protected operations
- [ ] Permission verified for action
- [ ] organizationId from session, not client input
- [ ] User input validated and sanitized
- [ ] No secrets in code or client bundle
- [ ] Audit log for sensitive operations
- [ ] File uploads validated (if applicable)
